schema: concept-lineage
object:
  title: "Decentralization"
  type: concept-lineage
  short_description: >-
    Spreading control and decision-making across many people instead of
    concentrating it in one place, so no single person, company, or office
    can shut things down, change the rules alone, or lock everyone else out.
    Framed in the article as a way of organizing power, not primarily a
    technology.
  source_traditions:
    - cooperatives
    - mutual aid networks
    - seed-saving circles / community seed swaps
    - rotating savings groups
    - crypto / DAO governance
  toolkit_usage: >-
    Used as a foundations-layer explainer for community organizers and
    permaculturists new to crypto — reframes "decentralization" away from
    crypto-hype jargon toward practices (seed swaps, shared tooling, group
    decision-making) the audience already runs, then bridges to crypto-native
    tools (Snapshot, quadratic voting) and ReFi DAO's Local Nodes model as
    concrete implementations.
  adjacent_meanings:
    - "technical/architectural decentralization (e.g. node distribution, consensus)"
    - "governance decentralization (who decides, per this article's framing)"
    - "economic decentralization (distributed ownership of value/tokens)"
  important_distinctions:
    - >-
      decentralization-is-a-spectrum-not-a-switch — most real systems are
      hybrids (e.g. an open, tamper-proof ledger paired with founder-driven
      day-to-day decisions); the honest question is "who holds power, over
      what, and could they abuse it?" not a binary yes/no.
    - >-
      transparency-is-not-optional — the article argues real decentralization
      comes with *more* transparency, not less, and treats "no one's in
      charge" framing without visible decisions/money/people as a scam signal.
  tensions:
    - >-
      openness-vs-founder-control — a project can have a fully open, immutable
      public ledger while day-to-day decisions still run through a small
      founding group; the article treats this as normal-but-worth-naming
      rather than a failure state.
  risks_of_flattening: >-
    Treating decentralization as purely a technical/architecture property
    (node count, consensus mechanism) erases the article's core claim that
    it is fundamentally about organizing power and accountability — a
    community garden with a rotating steering group can be meaningfully
    decentralized without touching a blockchain.
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/what-is-decentralization.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted

# assessed-but-excluded:
# - Coordinape's 2025 app wind-down: treated as a passing tool-churn example,
#   not extracted as its own resource/signal — too thin to stand alone without
#   more sourcing, folded into the claim-evidence entry on the spectrum framing
#   instead.
# - ReFi DAO Local Nodes mention: already covered as a source-system elsewhere
#   in this ingestion batch (what-is-refi.md work order); not re-registered
#   here to avoid a duplicate source-system card for the same entity.
